  "canon:pmid": "36902129",
  "canon:title": "beta-Adrenoreceptors as Therapeutic Targets for Ocular Tumors and Other Eye Diseases-Historical Aspects and Nowadays Understanding.",
  "canon:abstract": "beta-adrenoreceptors (ARs) are members of the superfamily of G-protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs), and are activated by catecholamines, such as epinephrine and norepinephrine. Three subtypes of beta-ARs (beta1, beta2, and beta3) have been identified with different distributions among ocular tissues. Importantly, beta-ARs are an established target in the treatment of glaucoma. Moreover, beta-adrenergic signaling has been associated with the development and progression of various tumor types. Hence, beta-ARs are a potential therapeutic target for ocular neoplasms, such as ocular hemangioma and uveal melanoma. This review aims to discuss the expression and function of individual beta-AR subtypes in ocular structures, as well as their role in the treatment of ocular diseases, including ocular tumors.",
  "canon:text": "beta-Adrenoreceptors as Therapeutic Targets for Ocular Tumors and Other Eye Diseases-Historical Aspects and Nowadays Understanding. beta-adrenoreceptors (ARs) are members of the superfamily of G-protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs), and are activated by catecholamines, such as epinephrine and norepinephrine. Three subtypes of beta-ARs (beta1, beta2, and beta3) have been identified with different distributions among ocular tissues. Importantly, beta-ARs are an established target in the treatment of glaucoma. Moreover, beta-adrenergic signaling has been associated with the development and progression of various tumor types. Hence, beta-ARs are a potential therapeutic target for ocular neoplasms, such as ocular hemangioma and uveal melanoma. This review aims to discuss the expression and function of individual beta-AR subtypes in ocular structures, as well as their role in the treatment of ocular diseases, including ocular tumors.",
